ture students to become industry leaders who will generate societal, economic and environmental benefits.\nOur study areas\nMechanical and advanced manufacturing engineering\nMechanical and advanced manufacturing engineering are intertwined at all points in the process. Everything we use on a daily basis is mechanically engineered, and at one time manufactured.\nopens in a new window\nRobotics and mechatronics engineering\nIn the age of smart machines, gain the specialised knowledge needed to create smarter innovative machines that increase human capability across industries, including health and manufacturing.\nopens in a new window\nSpace and Aerospace Engineering\nSpace and Aerospace Engineering involves design, manufacture, operations and development of flight vehicles and their systems. You\u2019ll have the choice and options to discover your own path.\nNuclear Engineering\nWhen you work in Nuclear Engineering you\u2019ll examine and leverage nuclear concepts which form the foundation of our universe.\nopens in a new window\nLatest news\nTrending topics\nSchool of Mechanical and Manufacturing Engineering\nUNSW experts awarded $32.8 million to drive ambitious research\nThe Australian Research Council (ARC) is supporting 28 academics from UNSW Sydney to undertake research advancing solutions to national priorities.\nSep 09, 2026\nEngineering\nScience\nArts, Design & Architecture (ADA)\nMedicine & Health\nDivision of Equity Diversity & Inclusion\nSDG\nSDG3\nSDG7\nSDG8\nSDG9\nSDG11\nSDG13\nScience & Tech\nGeneral\nResearch\nNetworks and partnerships\nEnvironments and systems\nSustainability\nProsperity\nHealthy lives\nResilient society\nSchool of Civil and Environmental Engineering\nSchool of Electrical Engineering and Telecommunications\nSchool of Mechanical and Manufacturing Engineering\nSchool of Photovoltaic and Renewable Energy Engineering\nSchool of Chemical Engineering\nSchool of Biomedical Engineering\nSchool of Psychiatry\nSchool of Population Health\nSchool of Biological, Earth & Environmental Sciences (BEES)\nSchool of Chemistry\nSchool of Mathematics and Statistics\nSchool of Physics\nSchool of Psychology\nSchool of Built Environment\nSchool of Education\nFertility innovation claims top prize in UNSW's 3 Minute Thesis\nPhD candidate Benjamin Wu won UNSW\u2019s 3 Minute Thesis competition with his presentation on reversing ageing of the human egg.\nSep 01, 2026\nDivision of Equity Diversity & Inclusion\nSDG\nSDG3\nSDG4\nSDG8\nSDG9\nEngineering\nScience\nMedicine & Health\nSchool of Biomedical Sciences\nSchool of Clinical Medicine\nSchool of Mechanical and Manufacturing Engineering\nSchool of Chemistry\nStudents\nGeneral\nEducation\nResearch\nUNSW experts secure international funding to advance fetal monitoring\nResearchers aim to replace decades-old fetal monitoring methods with advanced technology that could help clinicians make faster, more informed decisions during labour.\nJul 10, 2026\nEngineering\nMedicine & Health\nDivision of Equity Diversity & Inclusion\nScience & Tech\nGeneral\nSchool of Mechanical and Manufacturing Engineering\nSchool of Clinical Medicine\nSDG\nSDG3\nWomen engineers honoured as UNSW awards celebrate excellence and Ada Lovelace Medal winner\nJul 08, 2026\nSchool of Civil and Environmental Engineering\nSchool of Computer Science and Engineering\nSchool of Electrical Engineering and Telecommunications\nSchool of Mechanical and Manufacturing Engineering\nSchool of Photovoltaic and Renewable Energy Engineering\nSchool of Chemical Engineering\nSchool of Minerals and Energy Resources Engineering\nSchool of Biomedical Engineering\nEngineering\nEquity diversity & inclusion\nAll news\nResearch & impact\nOur research\nBy combining expertise and technological innovation, we continually strive to expand the boundaries of engineering. We are passionate about our research and collaborate with leading companies, locally and internationally to develop engineering solutions to complex problems.\nResearch groups\nOur research groups are at the forefront of developing pioneering technologies in mechanical and manufacturing processes. Our research has real-world impact, from developing new cancer treatments to revolutionising manufacturing processes. Explore our diverse research areas.\nFacilities\nWhether it's creating new processes or designing innovative products for the future, we provide our students with unparalleled experiences. From laser labs to flight simulators, our facilities and teaching spaces will expand your horizons as you work on world changing projects.\nWays to connect\nSign up to our newsletter for our latest news, featured stories, profiles and alumni Q&A.\nEnquire now\nTop\nunsw.edu.au\nEngage with us\nContact us\nFind an expert\nCareers at UNSW\nEducation at UNSW\nAlumni\nStudy\nUNSW International\nDegree Finder\nStudent Portal\nAcademic Calendar\nNews, Media & Events\nNewsroom\nUNSW events\nStudent news\nAbout us\nOur rankings & reputation\nFaculties and schools\nCampus locations\nCentres and institutes\nUNSW Library\nUNSW\nSydney NSW 2033 Australia\nTelephone: +61 2 93851000\nUNSW CRICOS Provider Code: 00098G\nTEQSA Provider ID: PRV12055\nABN: 57 195 873 179\nAcknowledgement of Country\nUNSW respectfully acknowledges the Bidjigal, Biripai, Dharug, Gadigal, Gumbaynggirr, Ngunnawal and Wiradjuri peoples, on whose unceded lands we are privileged to learn, teach and work. We honour the Elders of these Nations, past and present, and recognise the broader Nations with whom we walk together. UNSW acknowledges the enduring connection of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples to culture, community and Country.\nThe Uluru Statement\nFollow us\nFollow us\nFollow UNSW on LinkedIn\nFollow UNSW on Facebook\nFollow UNSW on Instagram\nFollow UNSW on TikTok\nFollow UNSW on YouTube\nFollow UNSW on WeChat\nUNSW Little Red Book\nPrivacy statement\nCopyright & Disclaimer\nAccessibility\nSite feedback\nComplaints", "links": [["Skip to Main Content", "https:/